why 1 3/4 LEADING_TONE The bass leaps G♯–C♯ to put the A chord in first inversion. The A that G♯ leads to arrives in the soprano on the next beat (B–A).
why 2 0 LEAPS The bass rises G♯–C♯–F♯ in fourths, roots moving by fifths toward the half cadence: a strong, purposeful bass.
why 2 1/4 PROGRESSION The bass climbs F♯–G♯–A–B to the dominant at the fermata; I⁶ is a passing chord on that climb, not a step backward.
why 3 3/4 DOUBLING Soprano and tenor meet on D♯ by step; the tenor goes on up to E at once, resolving it.
why 4 0 LEADING_TONE The tune falls D♯–C♯ here, and Bach keeps the hymn melody. The E that D♯ leads to comes in the tenor at the same moment.
why 5 0 OVERLAP The tune leaps up a fourth to E and the alto leaps up with it to C♯, above where the soprano was. They move together, so the order stays clear.
why 5 0 RANGE The new phrase starts high: the tenor leaps up to G♯ to stay close under the leaping tune. Bach's tenors sang up to A.
why 5 1/4 RANGE The tenor holds its high G♯ while the bass falls an octave.
why 5 1/4 SPACING The bass drops an octave to a low G♯ while the tenor stays high, giving the chord a deep base. The gap lasts a beat.
why 5 3/8 SPACING The same wide space between the high tenor and the low bass.
why 5 1/2 RANGE Still the high G♯; the tenor steps down F♯–E–D♯ over the next beats.
why 5 1/2 SPACING Still wide: the bass steps up to A while the tenor holds G♯.
why 5 5/8 SPACING The tenor starts down, F♯–E, and the gap narrows.
why 7 0 DOUBLING The A minor chord (iv, borrowed from E minor) has its third C in the bass and alto. In a minor chord the third is fine to double, and the chromatic C gives the passage its dark color.
why 7 1/4 TENDENCY The bass rises chromatically C–C♯–D–D♯–E toward the tonic: a slow, tense climb typical of the Passions. Each chromatic note pushes upward instead of falling.
why 7 1/2 DOUBLING Soprano (G♯–F♯) and tenor (E♯–F♯) meet on F♯ by step; the tenor's E♯ is a leading tone and must rise.
why 7 3/4 TENDENCY The chromatic climb goes on: D rises to D♯ and then to E, the tonic. The bass line decides the direction, not single notes.
why 8 0 DOUBLING Soprano (A–G♯) and tenor (F♯–G♯) meet on G♯ by step at the end of the bass's chromatic climb.
